<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marduk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marduk was the chief god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, elevated to head of the Mesopotamian pantheon as Babylon's power grew. His cult was central to the legitimacy of Babylonian kings under the Babylonian and Chaldean empires. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> presented his conquest of Babylon as the will of Marduk, and Achaemenid kings maintained his worship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marduk.jpg" alt="Marduk" width="320" height="555" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Heinrich Weißbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marduk_and_pet.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This tolerance for local institutions was a deliberate strategy: Cyrus framed the conquest of Babylon as the restoration of <b>Marduk</b>'s favour, and the Achaemenid kings consistently sought legitimacy within the religious frameworks of their subjects, ruling as pharaoh in Egypt and as restorer of religious order in Babylonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The religious dimension of government was equally important: Hammurabi's promotion of <b>Marduk</b>, Babylon's city god, whom the Code's prologue describes as granted dominion over humankind by the great gods, aligned divine authority with Babylonian political supremacy — a process that culminated centuries later in Marduk's recognition as king…</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ishtar Gate, the Processional Way, the rebuilt temple of <b>Marduk</b> with its ziggurat Etemenanki, the South Palace and the Hanging Gardens, whose existence is still debated, all reflect a state channelling surplus revenue into monumental architecture with deliberate ideological intent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat of the Kassite dynasty in the mid-twelfth century BCE ended more than four centuries of Kassite rule and created the conditions for the Babylonian revival under Nebuchadnezzar I, who later recovered the statue of <b>Marduk</b> from Susa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mesopotamian religion was polytheistic and also henotheistic: the people of a city held its tutelary god above the others, as Enki at Eridu, Enlil at Nippur, Ishtar at Arbela, <b>Marduk</b> at Babylon and Ashur at Assur.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Other works include the Enuma Elish, the Babylonian epic of creation, in which the god <b>Marduk</b> defeats the sea monster Tiamat and creates the world from her body; the Atrahasis epic, with its own account of the creation of humanity and the flood; the Descent of Ishtar to…</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
